{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/852741-help-with-some-werewolf-elements-in-my-hunter-chronicle","id":852741,"title":"Help with some werewolf elements in my hunter chronicle","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-03-24T18:58:35-07:00","id":852741,"title":"Help with some werewolf elements in my hunter chronicle","content":"Okay, so I'm running a Hunter game about the early days of Task Force Valkyrie set in 1881 Colorado Springs. I want to include some werewolves and spirits for them to hunt, but don't know a whole lot about WtF. The only ideas I really have right now are the spider spirits (Azlu?) infesting some abandoned gold mines and the Pure Tribes (probably the Fire Touched, if I'm interpreting things correctly) stirring up the local Native American tribes in an effort to weaken the gauntlet. <br /> <br /> That being said, I'm open to more ideas and I could definitely use some help fleshing out motivations for these guys.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2186-nerdynick","name":"Nerdynick","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2186_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-24T19:22:31-07:00","id":852753,"title":"","content":"Interesting; I could see the players pitting the two groups against each other.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-24T19:42:50-07:00","id":852760,"title":"","content":"As it so happens the primary location book for Werewolf was about the Rockies. It's a modern book but does delve into the past of the area but it's probably not worth suggesting you get it for a hunter game, though it is a nice book. One highlight they point at and you might look into are the Rocky Mountain locust, which existed in huge numbers around that time but was extinct just a couple decades afterward. Another is the building of the railroad, which the azlu took advantage of to weaken powerful, once-untouched territories of nature spirits and thicken the Gauntlet. It's suggested they might have directly changed some of the plans, having the railroads build in certain ways for their own mysterious reasons. For some reason railroads and trains would drive beshilu into a frenzy, as well, though it's unknown as to why.<br /> <br /> It also goes into the Azlu and Beshilu. Now, they're not spirits, they're something called hosts or shartha. Basically according to myth a long time ago, powerful Pangaean gods were killed by Father Wolf. One was the Plague King, a great rat that spread disease. The beshilu are the remnants of it, shards of its once great soul that exist in the form of rats. They devour other shards to grow more powerful, and sometimes burrow into the chests of people to control them like a marionette and, eventually, become a rat-human hybrid. From there things get more mutation-y. The beshilu exist to tear down the Gauntlet, but they also make allies among disease spirits, who trail along with their activities. The fiction in the Werewolf second edition core book details the struggle werewolves are having with a beshilu swarm and the devastating effect the beshilu can have on the health of an entire city when in power. The Azlu are descendants of the Spinner Hag, with a similar origin story. They crawl into peoples brains, instead of their hearts, and are devoted to thickening the Gauntlet, which can be just as bad a thing to an environment. Both species have a sort of end goal whereupon they create a messiah or manage to resurrect their progenitor. In the meantime, they are nasty opponents that come in a variety of sizes, from small animal shards that look like rats or spiders, to normal humans 'controlled' by them, to half-rat or half-spider hybrids in a variety of forms, and giant monster versions the size of cars or houses. When you defeat one, it generally divides up into a bunch of smaller shards, so you suddenly have a swarm of spiders fleeing everywhere that will eventually try to rejoin one another.<br /> <br /> Mechanically they're pretty simple, though, you can just make a Horror with Dread Powers aligned with poisons, venoms, webs or sickness. Throw in some physical abilities and you're set. They're great antagonists, since they have a lot of escape methods that allow them to survive what might seem like death, and come in a variety of forms. I used the three sample ones in the Predators book as antagonists in one of my hunter games. The cell managed to kill them but some of the spider shards hitched rides on them afterward and ended up taking over others the hunters led them to.<br /> <br /> Fire-Touched and Predator Kings are fairly traditional enemies, with the Fire-Touched being religious fanatics who can really find their way into any religion, and the Predator Kings hating the works and weaknesses of mankind. Neither would take kindly to people who saw themselves as hunters of the supernatural. The Ivory Claws, the third tribe, tend to be more wealthy and influential, and it would be easy to see some as a robber baron family or something. The other tribes of the Forsaken tend to focus in different areas. Hunters in Darkness tend to be very territorial, Blood Talons are big on combat, Storm Lords try to be strong and indomitable, the Bone Shadows focus on the Hisil and spirits, and the Iron Masters have an interest in change and civilization. Then there's the Bale Hounds, who in first edition at least, could be boiled down to essentially werewolves that served the demon-gods of the Nine Sins.<br /> <br /> I'm taking it you're using first edition?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 03-24-2016, 10:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-26T04:05:27-07:00","id":853421,"title":"","content":"What it sounds like is that you've got the Fire-Touched and the Azlu working against each other (as they tend to do) and your hunters are going to stumble right into the middle of it. Fun times.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-26T07:20:55-07:00","id":853457,"title":"","content":"Yes, I'm running this with 1st edition. <br /> <br /> Colorado Springs is a major railroad junction. With some anachronisms I've included, the town has 6 major rail companies running through it, if that has any spiritual significance (as you indicated it might to the Azlu). <br /> <br /> Having never really messed with these entities (vampires and ghosts are really more in my comfort zone as a ST), how many are my party reasonably capable of handling on their own, and are there any notable powers or weaknesses I should be aware of? Can a party of three deal with a single werewolf in war form (garou?)? How much preparation do they need to take these guys out? I remember from a few snatches at a friends WtF book (I have one coming in the mail), that the Pure are thought to be especially vulnerable to silver. (I was also thinking about turning the Azlu newt into a silver mine instead of a gold mine). Will silver bullets make any confrontation too easy?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2186,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2186-nerdynick","user":"Nerdynick","date":". 03-26-2016, 10:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2186-nerdynick","name":"Nerdynick","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2186_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-26T08:44:54-07:00","id":853505,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nerdynick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/852741-help-with-some-werewolf-elements-in-my-hunter-chronicle?p=853457#post853457\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, I'm running this with 1st edition.<br /> <br /> Colorado Springs is a major railroad junction. With some anachronisms I've included, the town has 6 major rail companies running through it, if that has any spiritual significance (as you indicated it might to the Azlu).<br /> <br /> Having never really messed with these entities (vampires and ghosts are really more in my comfort zone as a ST), how many are my party reasonably capable of handling on their own, and are there any notable powers or weaknesses I should be aware of? Can a party of three deal with a single werewolf in war form (garou?)? How much preparation do they need to take these guys out? I remember from a few snatches at a friends WtF book (I have one coming in the mail), that the Pure are thought to be especially vulnerable to silver. (I was also thinking about turning the Azlu newt into a silver mine instead of a gold mine). Will silver bullets make any confrontation too easy?</div> </div> </div> </div>Lunacy is pretty hard to beat. Even with assault rifles loaded in silver ammo, if your PCs get Lunacy'd hard, they'll be like lambs to the slaughter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-26T10:07:50-07:00","id":853546,"title":"","content":"The pure don't really react any differently to silver bullets than forsaken. The difference is that they get hurt simply by touching the stuff, and the forsaken don't - you have to actually <i>injure</i> them with it. <br /> <br /> Since you're using first edition your hunters will have an easier time fighting gauru-form werewolves. No super-regeneration to deal with there, or bullet dodging. Still wouldn't expect it to be an easy fight though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}